WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:20.756 --> 00:00:26.312
So networking minds is important because
it unlocks solutions that you would never

00:00:26.312 --> 00:00:31.882
come up with by yourself, and you would
also miss with other like-minded people.

00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:36.824
So the idea is if you can pull in
the brain power of diverse points of view,

00:00:36.824 --> 00:00:41.445
if you can harness cognitive diversity,
you can build solutions,

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:45.254
build companies, build outcomes,
create new ideas,

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:50.952
that otherwise would be unavailable to you
and the people immediately around you.

00:00:50.952 --> 00:00:55.375
There are different tools to network
minds, just like there's a conversation

00:00:55.375 --> 00:01:00.009
about what is open innovation, how do I
collaborate, and I'll outline a couple.

00:01:00.009 --> 00:01:04.632
[MUSIC]

00:01:04.632 --> 00:01:06.341
Prize competitions are not new.

00:01:06.341 --> 00:01:09.434
Charles Lindbergh won a prize for flight.

00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:11.610
Margarine, the fire extinguisher,

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.374
there are lots of different things that
came out of old school prize competitions.

00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:20.494
The new thing about price competitions
is the online platforms and forums.

00:01:20.494 --> 00:01:22.510
So I can put out there, hey,

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:27.382
I have $500 if anyone can solve
my problem, there's the prize.

00:01:27.382 --> 00:01:33.730
Or $1,000, or $50,000, or the x prize,
there's a huge prize competition.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:38.427
There's a company called InnoCentive,
that rolled out of Eli Lily, and

00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:42.687
this is for solving pharmaceutical
drug problems, originally.

00:01:42.687 --> 00:01:46.533
All of these individual
companies are trying,

00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:50.963
with their own scientists
to solve disease problems.

00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:56.241
And InnoCentive Alf Bingham said, no
let's have a big platform for seekers and

00:01:56.241 --> 00:02:00.908
solvers, those who are seeking
solutions and those who are solving.

00:02:00.908 --> 00:02:05.736
And there's research showing the farther
you are away from this expertise

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:08.889
the more likely you are to
solve these problems.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.271
So the kid in Romania,
the 15-year old, yeah,

00:02:12.271 --> 00:02:17.148
that might be the brainpower you need
to solve a problem that's sitting in

00:02:17.148 --> 00:02:20.629
a pharmaceutical company in Boston,
for example.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:25.926
Online collaboration and
awarding prizes has a bunch of reach.

00:02:25.926 --> 00:02:29.315
So if you want to network minds,
prize competition is one way.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:34.040
[MUSIC]

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.345
&gt;&gt; A flash team is a project team.

00:02:36.345 --> 00:02:40.555
You can come together work on
one project and then disband.

00:02:40.555 --> 00:02:45.373
This is enabled through all the online
communication that's out there in

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:46.547
different ways.

00:02:46.547 --> 00:02:50.084
You can pull the engineer for one project.

00:02:50.084 --> 00:02:52.891
Hourly Nerd is a company out of
Harvard Business School recently.

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:56.653
You can hire a nerd by the hour
to be a consultant for

00:02:56.653 --> 00:03:00.786
whatever project that you
have that you need to solve.

00:03:00.786 --> 00:03:05.640
And these flash teams, the idea at a big
company like IBM, we're seeing across

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.496
the entire company, people come together
in their smart cities initiative for

00:03:10.496 --> 00:03:14.215
a couple weeks, and
then go back to their traditional jobs.

00:03:14.215 --> 00:03:19.725
A flash team can come together, solve
a problem, if that working arrangement

00:03:19.725 --> 00:03:25.080
worked well, many of those people will
want to come and work together again.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:31.380
If it didn't, you disband and go on with
life, and go back to doing other teams.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.433
But this is enabled very much
as an online collaboration or

00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:37.510
a networking of brain power.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.471
[MUSIC]

00:03:42.471 --> 00:03:46.404
So a third way that you
could online reach out and

00:03:46.404 --> 00:03:51.132
grab smart minds,
Is through the gamification of work.

00:03:51.132 --> 00:03:56.337
So you can build a game, and
not just for entertainment,

00:03:56.337 --> 00:04:00.796
but for solving a problem or
building a product.

00:04:00.796 --> 00:04:05.680
If you can make it fun, you can often
get a lot of other people to play.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.926
And in the playing of games
together you build trust,

00:04:08.926 --> 00:04:12.327
you build relationships,
and you can crack problems.

00:04:12.327 --> 00:04:17.995
So an example there, out of
the University of Washington, is folded.

00:04:17.995 --> 00:04:22.050
So this is a protein folding game,
it's much like Tetris, and

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:27.194
human beings can see in three dimensions,
so this is a three dimensional game.

00:04:27.194 --> 00:04:33.510
The computer, just big data, things
cannot see three dimensions as well.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.051
So University of Washington set up a game,
saying,

00:04:36.051 --> 00:04:39.711
hey help us fold these proteins and
figure out all these different things.

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:44.371
And people played the game against
each other, and in two weeks they

00:04:44.371 --> 00:04:49.453
figured out one of the protein folding
things for the replication of HIV.

00:04:49.453 --> 00:04:52.016
It had taken years and
no one had figured that out.

00:04:52.016 --> 00:04:56.913
So in a game format, harnessing minds
from across the globe to play a game,

00:04:56.913 --> 00:05:01.918
these scientists have solved part of
a problem that they want to have solved.

00:05:01.918 --> 00:05:06.581
[MUSIC]

00:05:06.581 --> 00:05:10.691
If you think about physical space,
space can be a lever, and

00:05:10.691 --> 00:05:12.554
this is really important.

00:05:12.554 --> 00:05:17.925
In the room that you're sitting in,
in your office, or in a class room,

00:05:17.925 --> 00:05:22.680
if it is one person standing in front and
lines or rows of chairs,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.281
that is a one to many model, and
that is not networking minds.

00:05:27.281 --> 00:05:31.452
That is one mind going forth and
saying what it knows.

00:05:31.452 --> 00:05:36.466
If you instead configure space
to be round tables of three or

00:05:36.466 --> 00:05:40.590
four or five people, and
vertical use of walls.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.506
If you write on walls, three or
four or five people can see that, and

00:05:44.506 --> 00:05:47.626
people can brainstorm and
solve a problem together.

00:05:47.626 --> 00:05:51.739
If you write on your own little note
pad in front of you as we have with

00:05:51.739 --> 00:05:56.222
traditional education, and
in business note taking everything else,

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:58.659
only one person is really seeing that.

00:05:58.659 --> 00:06:03.397
So if you want to harness the brain
power of more than one person and

00:06:03.397 --> 00:06:07.872
certainly a group of people,
use three dimensional space,

00:06:07.872 --> 00:06:10.681
use small configurations of people,

00:06:10.681 --> 00:06:15.618
use different office cubicles
are not networking minds, right?

00:06:15.618 --> 00:06:20.157
Figure out how you can use space as
a lever to influence other people to bring

00:06:20.157 --> 00:06:21.536
their ideas forward.

00:06:21.536 --> 00:06:22.036
[MUSIC]

